SOAPBOX: It was a challenging experience, and one I am truly grateful for.
SOAPBOX: Special thanks to Philippe, LX2A, for once again making it possible to enjoy piloting the RadioLux superstation from the hills of northern Luxembourg.
SOAPBOX: **Lowlights:**
SOAPBOX: The 15m propagation path to North America suffered significantly from low MUF conditions at this latitude, resulting in 428 fewer QSOs, 77 fewer WPX prefixes, and 1,232 fewer QSO points compared to my 2025 effort, largely due to a 50% reduction in North American activity on the band. The 20m path deteriorated dramatically during the first night after only three hours of operation. In addition, severe QRN persisted almost continuously from Saturday morning through Sunday morning. An unstable cluster connection and a technical issue with the automatic PA system added further challenges, although the latter was resolved after a few hours.
SOAPBOX: **Highlights:**
SOAPBOX: The contest started strongly with 238 QSOs in the first hour while managing dual pileups on the 20m/40m combination. A carefully studied and score-optimized operating strategy was implemented successfully, resulting in a higher number of worked prefixes despite significantly poorer propagation conditions. In many respects, I believe the operating performance itself was better than in 2025. Particular emphasis was placed on accuracy and rate preservation, even while dealing with severe QRN and repeated lightning-related crashes caused by thunderstorms in the Brussels area, approximately 120 km away.
SOAPBOX: Many thanks to everyone who participated and helped make the event enjoyable. Special thanks to my host and to the contest committee for making this opportunity possible.
SOAPBOX: A more detailed write-up will follow on 3830 and Facebook.
SOAPBOX: 73,
SOAPBOX: Suad
